  "Question": "What fraction lies halfway between \\(\\dfrac15\\) and \\(\\dfrac13\\) on the number line?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{2}{15}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{4}{15}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{53}{200}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{8}{15}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Writing both endpoints with the same denominator makes their spacing easy to compare:\n\\[\n\\frac15=\\frac3{15},\\qquad \\frac13=\\frac5{15}.\n\\]\nThe fraction \\(\\frac4{15}\\) lies between them and is the same distance from each, because\n\\[\n\\frac4{15}-\\frac3{15}=\\frac1{15},\\qquad\n\\frac5{15}-\\frac4{15}=\\frac1{15}.\n\\]\nTherefore the halfway fraction is \\(\\frac4{15}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
